.TH MULTICAP 1 "November 2026" "multicap 3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
multicap \- high-performance multicast stream capture utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B multicap
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B multicap
captures multicast UDP streams and writes them to PCAP files or raw payload files.
It is designed for high-bitrate streams (>1.5 Gbps) using a threaded architecture
with large ring buffers to decouple network I/O from disk I/O.
.PP
It supports SMPTE 2022-7 seamless protection switching, capturing both
redundant network paths at once.
.PP
Frames are received with an AF_PACKET TPACKET_V3 memory-mapped ring, so PCAP
files hold the real Ethernet frames from the wire (link type Ethernet), with
every header exactly as received and nanosecond timestamps.
A kernel BPF filter passes only UDP to the chosen group and port.
A separate UDP socket holds the IGMP membership; it is never read.
multicap uses only standard Linux interfaces and works with any Ethernet
NIC and driver.
.PP
multicap needs CAP_NET_RAW (root, or file capabilities; see
.BR "PERFORMANCE TUNING" ).
.SH "SINGLE STREAM MODE"
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-multi\fR=\fIADDR\fR
Multicast group address to capture (e.g., 239.1.1.1).
Must be in the range 224.0.0.0 \- 239.255.255.255.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR \fIINTERFACE\fR
Network interface to capture on (e.g., eth0, ens18).
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-port\fR=\fIPORT\fR
UDP port number.
.TP
\fB\-\-pcap\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Write Ethernet frames to a nanosecond PCAP file. Use '\-' for stdout.
If the NIC strips 802.1Q tags, the tag is put back in each frame.
.TP
\fB\-\-raw\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Write raw payload to file, stripping IP/UDP/RTP headers.
Use '\-' for stdout. Useful for capturing MPEG-TS streams.
.SH "SMPTE 2022-7 MODE"
.TP
\fB\-7\fR
Enable SMPTE 2022-7 redundancy capture mode.
.TP
\fB\-\-redmulti\fR=\fIADDR\fR
Red path multicast address.
.TP
\fB\-\-redport\fR=\fIPORT\fR
Red path UDP port.
.TP
\fB\-\-redint\fR=\fIINTERFACE\fR
Red path network interface.
.TP
\fB\-\-bluemulti\fR=\fIADDR\fR
Blue path multicast address.
.TP
\fB\-\-blueport\fR=\fIPORT\fR
Blue path UDP port.
.TP
\fB\-\-blueint\fR=\fIINTERFACE\fR
Blue path network interface.
.TP
\fB\-\-redpcap\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Write red path packets to PCAP file.
.TP
\fB\-\-bluepcap\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Write blue path packets to PCAP file.
.SH "COMMON OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-duration\fR=\fISECS\fR
Capture duration in seconds. Default is 0 (unlimited).
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-buffer\fR=\fIMB\fR
Ring buffer size in megabytes. Default automatically uses up to 25% of free RAM.
In 2022-7 mode the size is shared by the captured paths.
.TP
\fB\-\-kring\fR=\fIMB\fR
Size of the kernel receive ring per path in megabytes (default 256).
Frames wait here until the capture thread copies them to the ring buffer.
.TP
\fB\-\-split\-gro\fR
Write each UDP datagram of a GRO aggregate as its own frame, with the
lengths, IP ID and checksums recomputed per datagram as the kernel does when
it splits such a packet. Without it, aggregates are written as received,
the same as
.BR tcpdump (1).
Needs Linux 6.2 or later. \fB\-\-raw\fR always splits aggregates.
.TP
\fB\-\-hwts\fR
Use NIC hardware receive timestamps. multicap enables timestamping of all
packets on the NIC (needs CAP_NET_ADMIN) and restores the previous setting on
exit. If the NIC or driver cannot do this, a warning is printed and software
timestamps are used.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress statistics output.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Show version information and exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help message and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Single Stream Capture"
.PP
Capture multicast stream to PCAP file:
.RS
.nf
multicap \-\-multi 239.1.1.1 \-i eth0 \-\-port 5000 \-\-pcap capture.pcap
.fi
.RE
.PP
Capture raw MPEG-TS stream for 60 seconds:
.RS
.nf
multicap \-\-multi 239.1.1.1 \-i eth0 \-\-port 5000 \-\-raw stream.ts \-d 60
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipe to tcpdump for live analysis:
.RS
.nf
multicap \-\-multi 239.1.1.1 \-i eth0 \-\-port 5000 \-\-pcap \- | tcpdump \-r \- \-nn
.fi
.RE
.SS "SMPTE 2022-7 Capture"
.PP
Capture both redundant paths:
.RS
.nf
multicap \-7 \\
  \-\-redmulti 239.20.1.35 \-\-redport 40000 \-\-redint eth20 \-\-redpcap red.pcap \\
  \-\-bluemulti 239.21.1.35 \-\-blueport 40000 \-\-blueint eth21 \-\-bluepcap blue.pcap
.fi
.RE
.PP
Capture for 30 seconds:
.RS
.nf
multicap \-7 \-d 30 \\
  \-\-redmulti 239.20.1.35 \-\-redport 40000 \-\-redint eth20 \-\-redpcap red.pcap \\
  \-\-bluemulti 239.21.1.35 \-\-blueport 40000 \-\-blueint eth21 \-\-bluepcap blue.pcap
.fi
.RE
.SH "STATISTICS DISPLAY"
During capture, multicap displays live statistics on stderr.
.SS "Single Stream Mode"
.RS
.nf
Captured: 1234567 | Written: 1234500 | Ring drops: 0 | Kernel drops: 0 | Rate: 1523.4 Mbps | Buffer: 2.3%
.fi
.RE
.SS "SMPTE 2022-7 Mode"
.RS
.nf
Red: 617283 (1.2%) | Blue: 617284 (1.1%) | Ring drops: 0 | Kernel drops: 0 | 3046.8 Mbps
.fi
.RE
.PP
Counts are frames. \fBRing drops\fR are frames lost because the ring buffer
was full (output too slow). \fBKernel drops\fR are frames lost because the
kernel receive ring was full (capture thread too slow); see \fB\-\-kring\fR.
The final summary also shows, per path, the number of UDP datagrams, how many
frames were GRO aggregates, bytes written, the drop rate and, with
\fB\-\-hwts\fR, how many frames had a hardware timestamp.
.PP
Buffer fill warnings are color-coded:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
>50%: Yellow "(buffer elevated)"
.IP \(bu 2
>75%: Bold yellow "WARNING: Buffer filling"
.IP \(bu 2
>90% or any ring drop: Bold red "DISK TOO SLOW - DROPPING PACKETS"
.IP \(bu 2
Any kernel drop: Bold red "KERNEL RING DROPS - capture CPU too slow"
.RE
.SH "PERFORMANCE TUNING"
.SS "Kernel Ring"
Use \fB\-\-kring\fR. The net.core.rmem sysctls have no effect: frames do
not pass through a socket buffer.
.SS "Network Interface"
.RS
.nf
sudo ethtool \-G eth0 rx 4096
.fi
.RE
.SS "Capabilities (instead of root)"
.RS
.nf
sudo setcap cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin,cap_sys_nice+ep /usr/local/bin/multicap
.fi
.RE
.PP
CAP_NET_RAW is required. CAP_NET_ADMIN is used by \fB\-\-hwts\fR and
CAP_SYS_NICE for the capture thread's real-time priority.
File capabilities are ignored on filesystems mounted nosuid.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success, no packets dropped.
.TP
.B 1
Error (invalid arguments, socket error, missing CAP_NET_RAW, write error).
.TP
.B 2
Success, but some packets were dropped.
.SH FILES
Output files are created in the current directory unless an absolute path is specified.
.SH NOTES
For streams exceeding 1.5 Gbps, NVMe storage is recommended.
For 3+ Gbps streams, consider using a RAM disk (/dev/shm or tmpfs) for captures.
.PP
SATA SSDs typically cannot sustain the write speeds required for 3 Gbps streams.
.PP
Many NICs and drivers merge consecutive datagrams of a stream into one large
frame (GRO). Such records show only the first RTP header in Wireshark; use
\fB\-\-split\-gro\fR to get one record per datagram.
